NAME={"Intro":"Introduction","Ind":"Inductive Reasoning","15.SG":"Solid Geometry as a Deductive System","MR":"Midterm Review","FR":"Final Review","11R":"Ch 11 Summary and Review (pp. 475 to 480) plus Algebra Review (pp. 481 to 482)"}
# course lesson -> (jacobs keys, beyond-Jacobs note or '', misconception)
M={
"1.1":(["Intro"],"","A figure that looks equal is equal. Surface it: students guess before measuring, then record guess vs. measured."),
"1.2":(["Ind"],"","Enough examples prove a rule. Surface it: have them predict 6-point regions as 32 before counting 31."),
"1.3":(["1.1"],"","Lines are segments with ends. Surface it: ask how many points a line has; anyone saying 'two' has the misconception."),
"1.4":(["1.2"],"","Angle size depends on ray length. Surface it: show the same angle with long and short rays and ask which is bigger."),
"1.5":(["1.3"],"","Any closed figure is a polygon (circles, curved sides). Surface it: sort card with a circle and a crossed figure."),
"1.6":(["1.4"],"","The compass is only for circles, not for transferring lengths. Surface it: ask them to copy a segment without a ruler."),
"1.7":(["1.5"],"","Every word can be defined. Surface it: dictionary chase for 'point' loops back on itself."),
"1.8":(["2.1"],"","The converse of a true statement is true. Surface it: 'If it's a square, it's a rectangle' flipped."),
"1.9":(["2.2"],"","A definition only has to work one way. Surface it: a 'kite' definition that also admits non-kites."),
"1.10":(["2.3"],"","A proof is a list of true facts in any order. Surface it: give shuffled proof lines to reorder."),
"1.11":(["2.4"],"","Indirect proof means proving the opposite is true. Surface it: ask what the contradiction contradicts."),
"1.12":(["2.5","2.6"],"","Theorems and postulates are the same kind of thing. Surface it: sort statements by 'proved or assumed'."),
"2.1":(["3.1"],"","Algebra steps don't need reasons. Surface it: solve an equation and name each property."),
"2.2":(["3.2"],"","Distance can be negative. Surface it: points at -3 and 5 on a ruler, compute both ways."),
"2.3":(["3.2"],"Coordinate midpoint formula is beyond Jacobs 3.2 (Jacobs gives the number-line midpoint).","Midpoint is the difference of the endpoints over 2. Surface it: endpoints -2 and 8."),
"2.4":(["3.3"],"","Read the wrong protractor scale. Surface it: an obtuse angle that reads 50 on the inner scale."),
"2.5":(["3.4"],"","Bisecting means drawing any line through the middle by eye. Surface it: ask how they know it's exact."),
"2.6":(["3.5"],"","Complementary angles must be adjacent. Surface it: two separate 30 and 60 angles; are they complementary?"),
"2.7":(["3.6"],"","Vertical means 'up and down'. Surface it: vertical angles drawn on a tilted X."),
"2.8":(["3.7"],"","Perpendicular lines must be horizontal and vertical. Surface it: a tilted right angle."),
"2.9":(["3.7","4.7"],"","Any arc width works for every step. Surface it: an arc smaller than half the segment."),
"2.10":(["4.7","6.2"],"","Parallel construction just means 'draw it looking parallel'. Surface it: ask which angle pair the copy made equal."),
"2.11":(["3.5","3.6"],"","Vertical angles are equal 'because they look it'. Surface it: require a theorem name per line."),
"2.12":(["1.4"],"","Construction art is decoration, not math. Surface it: ask which construction each step of the design used."),
"3.1":(["8.1"],"Motion postulates (per Euclid, ANSWERS.md sec. 1): state here, as explicit postulates, that translations, rotations and reflections exist and preserve distance, angle measure, collinearity and betweenness (a reflection swaps half-planes). They are assumed, not proved, and every later congruence-by-motion argument (3.12, 4.3, 4.4) cites them.","The image and preimage are the same figure. Surface it: label A and A' on a slide."),
"3.2":(["8.1"],"","A translation can turn the figure. Surface it: overlay a tilted image and ask if it's a translation."),
"3.3":(["8.2"],"","Reflecting moves each point the same distance in one direction. Surface it: reflect over a slanted line."),
"3.4":(["8.1","8.2"],"","Rotation is always about the origin. Surface it: rotate about a vertex of the figure."),
"3.5":(["8.2"],"","Order of reflections doesn't matter. Surface it: reflect over two crossing lines in both orders."),
"3.6":(["8.3"],"","Only one sequence of motions maps one figure to another. Surface it: collect different class sequences."),
"3.7":(["8.3"],"","Dilations are rigid motions. Surface it: measure a side after a dilation."),
"3.9":(["6.1"],"","A rectangle has diagonal lines of symmetry. Surface it: paper-fold a rectangle along a diagonal."),
"3.10":(["7.2"],"","Point symmetry and line symmetry are the same. Surface it: the letter N and the letter A."),
"3.11":(["8.4"],"","Any shape tessellates. Surface it: try regular pentagons."),
"3.12":(["8.3"],"Jacobs defines congruence by parts (4.2) and adds the isometry definition in 8.3, p. 312; this course leads with the isometry definition.","Congruent means 'same shape'. Surface it: two similar triangles of different size."),
"4.1":(["4.2"],"","Order of letters in a congruence statement doesn't matter. Surface it: triangle ABC ≅ triangle FED vs triangle ABC ≅ triangle DEF (use = only for lengths and measures)."),
"4.2":(["4.3"],"","Three matching parts of any kind is always enough. Surface it: SSA and AAA counterexample cards."),
"4.3":(["4.3"],"Jacobs takes SAS as Postulate 6 (p. 147); this course justifies it from rigid motions.","The included angle can be any angle. Surface it: mark a non-included angle and ask if SAS applies."),
"4.4":(["4.3"],"Jacobs takes ASA as Postulate 5 (p. 147); this course justifies it from rigid motions.","ASA and AAS are the same test. Surface it: mark which side is between the angles."),
"4.5":(["4.4"],"","You can use what you're proving as a reason. Surface it: a proof that cites the goal."),
"4.6":(["4.4"],"","CPCTC can be used before the triangles are proved congruent. Surface it: reorder a flawed proof."),
"4.7":(["4.5"],"","Base angles are the angles at the bottom of the picture. Surface it: an isosceles triangle drawn upside down."),
"4.8":(["4.5"],"","Equiangular and equilateral are separate facts to memorize. Surface it: ask which theorem gives each."),
"4.10":(["4.6"],"","SSS is a postulate (it is a theorem in this course). Surface it: ask what SSS was proved from (SAS plus isosceles theorems, p. 164)."),
"4.11":(["4.6"],"","SSA works if the sides are long. Surface it: build two triangles from the same SSA data."),
"4.12":(["4.7"],"","A construction is correct if it looks right. Surface it: name the congruence test behind each step."),
"4.13":(["4.7"],"","Old constructions were already proved. Surface it: ask why the bisector construction from 2.5 works."),
"4.14":(["4R"],"","Every proof needs triangles congruent first. Surface it: a proof solvable by vertical angles alone."),
"5.1":(["5.1"],"","Multiplying an inequality by a negative keeps the sign. Surface it: -2x < 6."),
"5.2":(["5.2"],"","The exterior angle equals the sum of the remote interior angles here. Surface it: ask why only 'greater than' is allowed before the parallel postulate."),
"5.3":(["5.3"],"","The longest side is next to the largest angle. Surface it: label a scalene triangle and ask for the side opposite."),
"5.4":(["5.4"],"","Any three lengths make a triangle. Surface it: 2, 3, 6 straws."),
"5.5":([],"Supplement: not in Jacobs. Kept and labeled Supplement on slide 1; proof route before the parallel postulate in Euclid ANSWERS.md sec. 3.","A wider angle always gives a longer side regardless of the other sides. Surface it: change a side length too."),
"5.6":(["6.2"],"","Parallel tests assume the lines are parallel first. Surface it: ask what is given and what is proved."),
"5.8":(["6.3"],"","The parallel postulate can be proved. Surface it: brief history of failed attempts (p. 225ff)."),
"5.9":(["6.4"],"","Same-side interior angles are equal. Surface it: measure them on a slanted transversal."),
"5.10":(["6.5"],"","Angle sum 180 is true on any surface. Surface it: preview the sphere (16.1) as a teaser."),
"5.11":(["6.5"],"","Exterior angle equals the adjacent interior angle. Surface it: label the remote interiors."),
"5.12":(["6.6"],"","HL works for any triangle. Surface it: an obtuse triangle with matching side and 'hypotenuse'."),
"5.13":(["11.6"],"Jacobs treats slope in 11.6 (Thms 52 to 53, p. 463); this course moves it earlier.","Perpendicular slopes are negatives, not negative reciprocals. Surface it: slope 2 and slope -2 on a grid."),
"5.14":(["11.6"],"Writing line equations is beyond Jacobs.","Perpendicular line uses the same intercept. Surface it: substitute the point back in."),
"5.15":(["6R"],"","Two-column is the only valid proof form. Surface it: accept a flowchart and a paragraph version."),
"6.1":(["7.1"],"","A square is not a rectangle. Surface it: quadrilateral family tree sort."),
"6.2":(["7.2"],"","Parallelogram diagonals are equal. Surface it: measure on a slanted parallelogram."),
"6.3":(["7.2"],"","Diagonals bisect the angles in every parallelogram. Surface it: measure on a non-rhombus."),
"6.4":(["7.3"],"","One pair of parallel sides is enough. Surface it: a trapezoid."),
"6.5":(["7.4"],"","A rhombus is a square. Surface it: a tilted rhombus with no right angles."),
"6.6":(["7.5"],"Kites are beyond Jacobs 7.5.","All trapezoids are isosceles. Surface it: a right trapezoid."),
"6.7":(["7.6"],"","The midsegment is half of any side. Surface it: which side is it parallel to?"),
"6.8":([],"Coordinate proof of quadrilaterals is beyond Jacobs (needed for G.QP.2).","Showing one pair of slopes equal proves a parallelogram. Surface it: a trapezoid on a grid."),
"6.9":(["7.1","14.1"],"","Exterior angle sum grows with sides. Surface it: walk the polygon on the floor."),
"6.10":(["14.1"],"","Every equilateral polygon is regular. Surface it: a rhombus."),
"7.1":(["9.1"],"","Area and perimeter grow together. Surface it: same perimeter, different areas with string."),
"7.2":(["9.2"],"","Area units are linear units. Surface it: ask for the units on a 3 cm by 4 cm answer."),
"7.3":(["9.3"],"","The height is always a side. Surface it: an obtuse triangle with an outside altitude."),
"7.4":(["9.4"],"","Parallelogram area uses the slanted side. Surface it: shear a paper parallelogram into a rectangle."),
"7.5":(["9.1","9.2","9.3","9.4"],"","Overlapping regions get counted once automatically. Surface it: an L-shape split two ways."),
"7.6":(["9.5"],"","a squared plus b squared equals c squared for any triangle. Surface it: test an obtuse triangle."),
"7.7":(["9.5"],"Pythagorean fractal is beyond Jacobs.","The converse is the same theorem. Surface it: ask what is given in each direction."),
"7.9":(["4.1"],"Jacobs states the distance formula in 4.1 before proving Pythagoras; this course develops it after 9.5.","Distance formula is a new fact to memorize. Surface it: draw the right triangle on the grid first."),
"7.10":(["4.1"],"Perimeter and area on the coordinate plane extends Jacobs 4.1.","Count diagonal grid squares as 1 unit. Surface it: a diagonal side of length root 2."),
"7.11":(["4.1"],"Coordinate proof is beyond Jacobs.","One numeric example is a proof. Surface it: compare a specific example with variable coordinates."),
"7.12":(["4.1"],"Coordinate proof is beyond Jacobs.","Placement of axes doesn't matter for ease. Surface it: compare a corner-at-origin set-up with a random one."),
"8.1":(["10.1"],"","Proportions can be cross-multiplied with any pairing. Surface it: set up a ratio with mismatched parts."),
"8.2":(["10.5"],"Jacobs uses dilations as motivation in 10.5; this course defines similarity by them.","A dilation adds a fixed amount to lengths. Surface it: scale factor 2 on a 3-4-5 triangle."),
"8.3":(["10.5"],"","Dilations change angles. Surface it: measure angles before and after."),
"8.4":(["10.2"],"Jacobs defines similarity by correspondence (10.2); this course defines it by transformations.","Similar means 'roughly the same shape'. Surface it: two rectangles of different proportions."),
"8.5":(["10.2"],"","Corresponding sides are found by position on the page. Surface it: a rotated similar polygon."),
"8.6":(["10.3"],"","The side-splitter ratio uses the parallel segment. Surface it: label which pieces are proportional."),
"8.7":(["10.3"],"Partitioning a segment by coordinates is beyond Jacobs.","A 2:3 ratio means the point is 2/3 of the way. Surface it: ask for 2 parts of 5."),
"8.9":(["10.4"],"","You need all three angles. Surface it: ask why two are enough (the angle sum)."),
"8.10":(["10.5"],"","SSA similarity works. Surface it: counterexample card."),
"8.11":([],"Supplement: not a numbered Jacobs theorem (Euclid searched). Closest Jacobs item: 10.3, p. 397, Set II, ex. 48. Label Supplement on slide 1; needed for G.T.1f.","The bisector cuts the opposite side in half. Surface it: measure on a scalene triangle."),
"8.12":(["10.6"],"","Area scales by k, not k squared. Surface it: tile a doubled square."),
"8.13":(["10.6"],"Self-similar fractals are beyond Jacobs 10.6.","Every repeated pattern is self-similar. Surface it: a tiled floor vs. a Sierpinski triangle."),
"8.14":(["10.4"],"","Shadow lengths aren't proportional at different times. Surface it: ask why both shadows must be measured at once."),
"9.1":(["11.1"],"","The geometric mean is the average. Surface it: 4 and 9 give 6, not 6.5."),
"9.2":(["11.2"],"","A second proof of a theorem is pointless. Surface it: ask what the similarity proof uses that the area proof did not."),
"9.3":(["11.3"],"","The hypotenuse is 2 times the leg. Surface it: measure a 45-45-90 drawn on grid paper."),
"9.4":(["11.3"],"","The root 3 goes on the hypotenuse. Surface it: ask which side is opposite the 60 degree angle."),
"9.5":(["11.4"],"","Trig ratios depend on the triangle's size. Surface it: compare tan 30 in two sizes."),
"9.6":(["11.4"],"","Opposite and adjacent are fixed sides. Surface it: switch the reference angle."),
"9.7":(["11.5"],"","Sine can exceed 1. Surface it: ask which side is always the longest."),
"9.9":(["11.4","11.5"],"","sin inverse means 1 over sin. Surface it: compare calculator outputs."),
"9.10":(["11.6"],"","Slope and angle are proportional. Surface it: slope 1 is 45 degrees, slope 2 isn't 90."),
"9.11":(["11.4","11.5"],"","Angle of depression is measured from the vertical. Surface it: draw the horizontal line first."),
"9.12":(["11R"],"","Always reach for Pythagoras first. Surface it: problems where only one angle and one side are known."),
"9.13":(["11.7"],"","The Law of Sines always gives one triangle. Surface it: the ambiguous case (Euclid to confirm Jacobs' coverage)."),
"10.1":(["12.1"],"","A chord and a radius are the same. Surface it: label card sort."),
"10.2":(["12.1"],"","Any line through a chord's midpoint passes through the center. Surface it: it must also be perpendicular."),
"10.3":(["12.2"],"","A tangent can touch at two points. Surface it: drag a line across a circle."),
"10.4":(["12.3"],"","Arc measure is arc length. Surface it: same arc degrees on two circles."),
"10.5":(["12.4"],"","An inscribed angle equals its arc. Surface it: measure both."),
"10.6":(["12.4","13.2"],"","Any quadrilateral can be inscribed in a circle. Surface it: try a non-isosceles trapezoid."),
"10.7":(["12.5"],"Present as a corollary of earlier circle-angle theorems (Euclid ANSWERS.md): tangents PA, PB give right angles OAP and OBP (12.2), the quadrilateral angle sum (Ch 7) gives angle APB + angle AOB = 180, so angle APB = 180 minus the minor arc AB.","A circumscribed angle equals the arc. Surface it: measure."),
"10.9":(["12.5"],"","Every secant angle is half the sum of arcs. Surface it: vertex inside vs. outside."),
"10.10":(["12.6"],"","Segment products use whole lengths for chords. Surface it: label the pieces."),
"10.11":(["13.1"],"","The circumcenter is always inside. Surface it: an obtuse triangle."),
"10.12":(["13.3"],"","Incenter and circumcenter are the same point. Surface it: construct both on one triangle."),
"10.13":(["13.4"],"","Medians and altitudes are the same segment. Surface it: a scalene triangle."),
"10.14":(["13.6","13.5"],"","Surprising coincidences are luck. Surface it: drag the triangle and watch Napoleon's triangle stay equilateral."),
"11.1":(["14.2"],"","Perimeter of a regular polygon needs every side measured. Surface it: give one side and n."),
"11.2":(["14.3"],"","The apothem is the radius. Surface it: label both on a hexagon."),
"11.3":(["14.4"],"","Pi equals 22/7 or 3.14 exactly. Surface it: compare the polygon perimeters as n grows."),
"11.4":(["14.4"],"","C = pi r. Surface it: measure a can with string."),
"11.5":(["14.5"],"","Doubling r doubles area. Surface it: tile two circles."),
"11.6":(["14.6"],"","Arc length equals arc degrees. Surface it: same 60 degree arc on two radii."),
"11.7":(["14.6"],"","Segment area equals sector area. Surface it: subtract the triangle visibly."),
"11.8":(["14.6"],"Core, not enrichment: Jacobs 14.6, p. 608, heading The Radian, Set I ex. 26 to 30 (PDF p. 628). Derive angle = s/r, 180 degrees = pi radians.","Radians are a different kind of angle. Surface it: lay radius-length string along the arc."),
"12.1":(["15.1"],"","Two lines that don't meet are parallel. Surface it: skew lines with two pencils."),
"12.2":(["15.SG","15.2","15.3"],"Nets and cross sections extend Jacobs 15.2 to 15.3.","A cube's cross section is always a square. Surface it: the hexagon slice."),
"12.3":(["15.4"],"","Slanted stacks have more volume. Surface it: shear a stack of coins."),
"12.4":(["15.4","15.6"],"","Volume of a cylinder uses the diameter. Surface it: units check."),
"12.5":(["15.5","15.6"],"","A pyramid is half a prism. Surface it: pour rice from pyramid into prism."),
"12.6":(["15.7"],"","Sphere volume is 4 pi r squared. Surface it: units check (cubic vs. square)."),
"12.8":(["15.3","15.5","15.6","15.7"],"","Surface area and volume are interchangeable. Surface it: units on each."),
"12.9":([],"Composite solids with algebraic expressions (G.TS.4) are beyond Jacobs exercises; Euclid to confirm.","Shared faces count toward surface area. Surface it: two stacked cubes."),
"12.10":(["15.8"],"","Volume scales by k squared. Surface it: build a doubled cube from unit cubes."),
"12.11":(["15.9"],"","There are infinitely many regular polyhedra. Surface it: try to build one from hexagons."),
"12.14":(["16.1"],"","Straight lines on a sphere are like latitude lines. Surface it: string on a ball between two cities."),
}
